<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Charles de Gaulle</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1890">1890</a>–<a href="/years/1970">1970</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as De Gaulle</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Charles de Gaulle was a French general and statesman who led the Free French during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> and later founded the Fifth Republic, serving as president from 1959 to 1969. Free French forces relied heavily on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>'s African colonies. As president he oversaw the independence of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algeria">Algeria</a> and most of France's African colonies, ending the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-colonial-empire">French Empire</a> as a colonial system.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/charles-de-gaulle.jpg" alt="Charles de Gaulle" width="279" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Office of War Information, Overseas Picture Division. [1] … · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:De_Gaulle-OWI_(cropped)_(c)(2).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Charles André Joseph Marie de Gaulle was born on 22 November 1890 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lille">Lille</a>, the third of five children. He was raised in a devoutly Roman Catholic, conservative and monarchist family. His father, Henri de Gaulle, was a professor of history and literature at a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuit</a> college and later founded his own school.</p><p>In 1909 he won a place at the military academy of Saint-Cyr, finishing 119th out of 221. Before entering he served a year in the ranks in the 33rd Infantry Regiment at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arras">Arras</a>. His company commander declined to make him a sergeant at first, remarking that the young man evidently thought nothing less than Constable of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> good enough for him.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> he was wounded in the knee at the Battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dinant">Dinant</a> on 15 August 1914 and later taken prisoner by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-empire">German Empire</a>. After the armistice he served on the French military mission to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> and won the Polish Virtuti Militari. In the interwar years he advocated mobile armoured divisions.</p><p>When the German invasion came in May 1940 he led an armoured division in a counterattack and was appointed Undersecretary for War. Refusing his government's armistice with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, he went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> and urged the French to fight on in his Appeal of 18 June. He led the Free French Forces and in June 1944 became head of the Provisional Government of the French Republic. He resigned in 1946 and withdrew to Colombey-les-Deux-Églises to write his War Memoirs.</p><p>During the May 1958 crisis, as the Algerian War threatened the Fourth Republic, the National Assembly brought him back to power. He commissioned a new constitution and was elected President of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> on 21 December 1958. He withdrew France from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nato">NATO</a>'s integrated military command and launched an independent nuclear force, and on 22 January 1963 he signed the Élysée Treaty with Chancellor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/konrad-adenauer">Konrad Adenauer</a>. He resigned in late April 1969 after losing a referendum on decentralisation.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>De Gaulle died suddenly on 9 November 1970 from an abdominal aortic aneurysm while watching television. His successor Georges Pompidou announced his death by saying simply, &quot;General de Gaulle is dead. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> is a widow.&quot; French political parties and leaders claim a Gaullist legacy, and streets and monuments in France and elsewhere bear his name.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Charles de Gaulle” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Cross of Lorraine, adopted by René II, later became the emblem of the Free French under <b>Charles de Gaulle</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-lorraine">Duchy of Lorraine · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>…at Algiers and Oran in Operation Torch, and in 1943 Algiers became the seat of the French Committee of National Liberation, headed jointly by <b>Charles de Gaulle</b> and Henri Giraud until de Gaulle took sole control; Algerian units then fought in Italy and in the liberation of France.</blockquote><a href="/empire/french-algeria">French Algeria · Military</a></li><li><blockquote><b>De Gaulle</b>'s French Community of 1958 offered autonomy within a French framework.</blockquote><a href="/empire/french-empire">French Empire · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>On 10 July 1940 the French parliament gave full powers to Marshal Philippe Pétain, who proclaimed the French State at Vichy, while <b>Charles de Gaulle</b> called on the French to rally to Free France.</blockquote><a href="/empire/french-third-republic">French Third Republic · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>In the referendum of 28 September 1958 on <b>Charles de Gaulle</b>’s French Community, Guinea under Ahmed Sékou Touré voted no and became independent on 2 October 1958; the other territories voted yes and became autonomous republics within the Community.</blockquote><a href="/empire/french-west-africa">French West Africa · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>The empire slipped away from 1940, as French Equatorial Africa, Cameroun and the Pacific colonies rallied to <b>Charles de Gaulle</b>'s Free France.</blockquote><a href="/empire/vichy-france">Vichy France · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lille">Lille</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. Died in Colombey-les-Deux-Églises, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=50.63,3.06&amp;year=1942" aria-label="Lille on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Charles de Gaulle"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5166 5794h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5166 5794h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=50.63,3.06&amp;year=1942">What was here in 1942? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
